 of the pot if the opponent made a weak hand. Checking is the right move here, so I check to minimize potential losses. My goal is to preserve my lead.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: I hold 5♦ 2♥ on a board of Q♣ K♠ 3♣ A♠ 6♠. This means I am "playing the board" with an Ace-high hand (A-K-Q-6-3), as my private cards are lower than the community cards. I have no pair, straight, or flush. The opponent has checked every street (Pre-flop, Flop, Turn, River). While this indicates weakness, they could easily hold a low pair or a single high spade that beats the board. Because I checked the Flop and Turn, a sudden bet on the River looks like a bluff. Since I am currently winning the overall match (Hand 14/20) and the pot is small, there is no need to risk chips on a low-probability bluff. Checking back ensures I lose zero additional chips if beaten.\n\nReason: My hand has no showdown value, but the pot is small and I am protecting a lead in the tournament. Checking minimizes risk.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
